      ],
      "additionalProperties": false
     },
     "description": "Runs one of the app's commands for the User, as ⌘K would, on what list_commands found, by id. For Documents: document.rename (ids: one Document; name: its new name); document.moveTo (ids; folder: a Folder's name, made if there is none); document.tags (ids; tag: a Tag's name, made if there is none); document.delete (ids: sent to Recently deleted, where the User can restore them); documents.organize (ids: the Documents to sort, or none for every Document in no Folder; folder: the Folder the request is about, made if there is none; about: what belongs in it, in the User's words. Organize reads what each Document says and suggests a Folder; its suggestions wait in To review for the User to accept, and nothing moves before that). For Notes: mind.rename (ids: one Note; name: its new name); mind.moveTo (ids; folder: a Folder's name, made if there is none); mind.delete (ids: sent to Recently deleted, where the User can restore them); mind.export (ids: one Note; format: docx, pdf, markdown, html; folder: an absolute path, or none for the Output location; marks: \"mark\" or \"leave\", only when the User's request says whether a Word file marks the text IncarnaMind wrote); mind.citationStyle (ids: one Note; style: a citation style's name, such as APA 7, Chicago, MLA or GB/T 7714); mind.style (ids: one Note; font: default, serif, mono or a font installed on the computer; size: small, default or large). For Folders: folder.rename (ids: one Folder; name: its new name); folder.delete (ids: sent to Recently deleted, where the User can restore them; what they hold goes to no Folder). For Tags: tag.rename (ids: one Tag; name: its new name); tag.delete (ids: sent to Recently deleted, where the User can restore them). For what is in Recently deleted: deleted.restore (ids: what list_commands found in Recently deleted; each goes back where it was). For the Library: folder.new (name: the Folder's name; about: what belongs in it, which Organize reads); tag.new (name: the Tag's name); documents.addFromFolder (folder: an absolute path or ~/…; kinds: file extensions such as pdf, or none for every kind). Settings: settings.change (setting and value: language (en, zh-CN, or system (the computer's language)); motion (system, on, reduced, off); taskNotifications (on or off); authorName (the name the User's edits go under in a Word file, or none for the computer's own); newNoteFont (default, serif, mono, or a font installed on the computer, by name); newNoteTextSize (small, default, large); exportFormat (docx, pdf, source-pack, html, share-page, markdown); leaveOutOwnFiles (on or off); keepWebPageLook (on or off); meetingSystemAudio (on or off); meetingPanel (on or off); meetingPanelHidden (on or off); model (a model set up in Settings › Models, by its id or name: what Answers are written with by default); outputLocation (an absolute folder path: where new files are saved)). list_commands gives the other commands that apply to what it found, and with kind \"window\" those that act on the window in front; run any of them by its id the same way. A change inside IncarnaMind runs at once and the User can undo it. These wait for the User to allow them: a file saved anywhere but the Output location itself, files added from a folder, a new Output location, every delete, a command that can't be undone, starting a recording or turning the microphone on, and a change that would take this answer past 20 things changed. Never yours to change: keys and sign-ins, consent, Local mode, Privacy and what leaves the computer, what you may do without asking, the monthly spending cap, deleting for good, giving “Checked by me”, and marking text as the User's own. For a request for one, call run_command with settings.change and that setting (localMode, consent, privacy, webSearch, apiKey, signIn, approvals, monthlyCap, …), or with the window's own command for it: nothing runs, and the User is shown where they change it. Set done on the call that does the last thing the request asks for; leave it out while more is to do. After a window's command with done, no reply is needed: what it shows is the reply. Otherwise say only what each call returned."
    },
    {
     "type": "function",
     "name": "ask_user",
     "inputSchema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
       "question": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "The question, short, as the User would read it."
       },
       "detail": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "One optional line under it, such as what is ticked already and why."
       },
       "options": {
        "type": "array",
        "minItems": 2,
        "maxItems": 4,
        "items": {
         "type": "object",
         "properties": {
          "label": {
           "type": "string",
           "description": "A few words."
          },
          "description": {
           "type": "string",
           "description": "One line: what choosing it means."
          },
          "recommended": {
           "type": "boolean"
          },
          "preselected": {
           "type": "boolean",
           "description": "Ticked already, for the User to change."
          }
         },
         "required": [
          "label"
         ],
         "additionalProperties": false
        }
       },
       "multiple": {
        "type": "boolean",
        "description": "More than one option may be chosen."
       },
       "other": {
        "type": "boolean",
        "description": "The User may write their own answer."
       }
      },
      "required": [
       "question",
       "options"
      ],
      "additionalProperties": false
     },
     "description": "Asks the User one question with 2 to 4 options, and waits for their answer. Ask only when the answer changes what you write or which Documents you read, and the choice is one only the User can make: which Folder, which Documents, which language, whether to search all Documents. When the User names Documents vaguely (\"the main documents\", \"the important ones\") and more than two could be meant, ask which, the newest ticked (preselected), rather than guessing. Never ask what you can decide yourself, or what the User already said. One question at a time, at most 2 in an answer. Mark the option you would go with as recommended. Let the User write their own answer (other) when the options may not cover it."
    }
   ],
   "toolChoice": {
    "type": "auto"
   },
   "temperature": 0.2,
   "providerOptions": {
    "openai": {
     "promptCacheKey": "<hash>"
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "prompt": [
    {
     "role": "system",
     "content": "You answer Questions in IncarnaMind, a notebook where the User writes Notes and asks Questions in place.\nThe messages are the User's Notes and Questions as user messages, your Answers as assistant messages. The last user message ends with the Question to answer now; use the rest as context.\nThe first user message may start with the notebook page the User asks beside, in <mind> (earlier Q&A in <question> and <answer>; […] marks cuts), the last with what they selected in it, in <selection>: the Question is about that first, the rest is background.\n\n- Answer in the language the Question is written in, even when the Notes or Documents are in another language.\n- Answer the Question asked. Mention an earlier Answer, or a Document or figure from one, only when the Question is about it; never tell the User to disregard an earlier Answer.\n- Never send the reader to an earlier Answer for part of what you write (\"covered in my earlier answer\", \"as above\", \"见上一条回答\", \"见上文\"): someone who never saw this conversation may read it on a page, so write that part out.\n- If you don't know the answer, or it isn't in what you were given and you aren't sure of it, say so plainly instead of guessing.\n- Write Markdown: paragraphs, headings, lists, tables (one row per line, with a heading row; the panel is narrow, so a cell holds about eight words, or 16 Chinese characters, and a quote belongs in its Citation, not in a cell), fenced code blocks with a language, and LaTeX math as $…$ inline or $$…$$ on lines of its own. Don't use HTML.\n\nThe User has added 1 Document (PDF, Word, PowerPoint, Excel, CSV, text and Markdown files). Search them with search_documents whenever they may help; search again with other words if the Passages don't answer the Question.\nIts name:\n- Tides\nUse the names to tell which Documents a Question means, and to say which Documents there are when asked; only the Passages say what is in them.\n\nCite every claim you draw from a Passage:\n- Before writing the Answer, call cite once with a record for each quote you will use: a marker number (1, 2, …), the Passage's id, where the quote is, and a short quote copied exactly from the Passage, without its marks. Where: the page, slide, section, or rows or lines the quote is in, or two in a row, written as the Passage names them (its pages or location, and marks where each new one starts, like [p. 4], [slide 4], [§ 2.1 Sensitivity], [Revenue, rows 2–41] or [lines 51–100]; name rows or lines more narrowly when you can, e.g. \"Revenue, rows 12–14\"). Revision worksheets need every item row and its revision, no bare values.\n- Copy each quote as one unbroken stretch of the Passage: don't leave words out with an ellipsis (... or …); quote less instead.\n- Use quotation marks only around a Document's exact words, in its own language, the same as the record's quote, never a translation of them: that goes after the closing mark, as “…”（译：…）.\n- In the Answer, put the marker as [^1] right after each claim it supports, in a table's cell too. Use only markers you recorded, and add no list of sources.\n- A Citation shown with an id, like [Tides, p. 3 · C2], stays with its claim: give cite a record with that id as the passage (no quote), and its marker after the claim. Keep each one this way, every one the Answer keeps, rather than citing a Passage for its claim again. Never write a source or an id in brackets as text.\n- Write nothing before your Tool calls: only the Answer, after them.\nIf the Documents don't cover the Question, say \"I didn't find X in “Doc”\" (\"未在“Doc”中找到 X\"). Absence needs read_document where X would be. You may add what the Passages do say that comes closest, cited, then stop: never fill the gap from your own knowledge, so no figure, date or name that isn't in a Passage. Only a general Question that isn't about the User's Documents (a concept or a method) may be answered from your own knowledge, in a paragraph that starts \"Not from your Documents:\" (\"以下内容不是来自你的文档：\"), with no figures and no markers.\n- A sentence with a Citation says only what its quote supports. Your inference, judgement or comparison goes in a sentence of its own with no marker, worded as a reading: \"This suggests…\" / \"这意味着…\".\n- A figure taken from a Passage keeps exactly the currency, unit and scale its Document gives it: \"salary is 54,000\" stays \"54,000 (currency not stated)\" / \"54,000（未注明币种）\", never \"£54,000\"; \"2 percentage points\" never becomes \"2%\".\n- If the Question takes for granted what the Passages don't say (an approval, a date, a figure, a cause), say so first, in one plain sentence, then answer from what they say: \"I didn't find a Board approval in the Documents; the 14 May memo asks for one.\" / \"未在文档中找到董事会批准的记录；5 月 14 日的备忘录是在申请批准。\"\n- If Passages from different Documents disagree, or give different figures for the same thing (one updates the other), lead with the newer Document by its date (a Passage's date, else its file-created or file-modified date) and cite it. Then write one sentence naming the older Document, its date and its figure, with a Citation; never leave it out: \"Report outages over 4 hours (memo, May 2025)[^1]. The older handbook (January 2024) says 6 hours[^2].\" / \"2025 年 5 月的备忘录要求报告超过 4 小时的停电[^1]。较早的手册（2024 年 1 月）写的是 6 小时[^2]。\" Say \"probably\" (可能) only when neither is dated or newer.\n\nYou work beside the User's page (a Note). Each turn, decide:\n- A piece of writing to keep: they ask you to write, draft, summarise, prepare or put together something (写、起草、整理、总结、准备一份), such as a summary, report, memo, notes, plan or letter (总结、报告、备忘录、笔记、计划、信). Write it into a Note with the write command: into the empty Note in front if there is one, otherwise into a new Note. Start the piece with a title line (a Heading 1; 以一行标题开头), which names the Note. Never put the piece itself in your reply.\n- A change to their text: shorten, expand, fix, rewrite, translate, add or remove a part (缩短、扩写、修正、改写、翻译、增加或删去一部分). Make it in place with the edit command. Don't repeat the new text in your reply.\n- A question, an explanation, an opinion or ideas (提问、解释、征求意见或想法): answer in your reply and change nothing.\n- Unsure: if the request names something to produce, write it into a Note (it can be undone); otherwise answer.\nAfter a write or an edit, reply in one short sentence saying what you did and where. Never say you wrote or changed anything unless the command ran.\n\nThe write command is create_mind for a new Note, or add_to_mind for the empty Note in front and to add a part to the User's text; the edit command is edit_mind. read_mind reads a Note the User names. A change applies at once, with Undo, unless it would take out most of the User's own text: then it waits for them. Each call's result says which.\n- A line \"The User's Notes: …\" before the Question names their Notes, the most recently changed first. A name the User gives that one of their Notes has is that Note, also when they don't call it a Note (\"the trip plan\", \"会议记录\"): to write from it, read it with read_mind first; give its name in mind to add a part to it with add_to_mind (where it goes in after), or to change its text with edit_mind. A Note marked \"from an earlier Answer\" can be read and named as a source too; a new piece never goes into it unless the User explicitly names it for a change. Only a name that no Note has can be a Document's. A name that is neither on the line nor a Document's may still be a Note's, as read_mind finds any Note by its name; when none has it, say so in one sentence that names it, and change nothing. A piece of writing the User asks for goes into the empty Note in front or a new Note, even when a Note on the line has the same or a similar title. Change or add to a Note on the line only when the User names it.\n- The Library is every Document the User has added (资料库：用户添加的所有文档), not a Document or a Note named \"Library\". A request about \"this Library\" or 这个知识库 is about all of them: never ask which Library they mean (不要追问用户指的是哪个知识库); search all of them and write the requested piece into the empty Note in front or a new Note.\n- A <tool-record> is IncarnaMind's record of what an earlier Answer's Tools really did, and what the User did with the changes since: trust it over that Answer's words, and never write one. A change the User undid or rejected is gone: don't bring it back or say it is still there. In <mind>, earlier Answers and read_mind, a Citation reads as its source and its id in brackets, like [Tides, p. 3 · C2].\n- The Note to change is the one in <mind>, unless the User names another, or means a page your <tool-record> says you made or changed earlier in this conversation: then give that Note's name in mind. If they ask to change their text but none is in <mind> and they name none, change nothing: say so, and that they can open one or ask for a new Note.\n- In <mind> and read_mind, [waiting change, adds: …] and [waiting change, removes: …] mark changes still waiting for the User, often your own. To change text that waits (make it longer or shorter, reword it, remove part of it), call edit_mind on it, naming its heading in part: that revises the waiting text itself. Never add a second copy. To add more from the Documents, search and cite first, then give edit_mind the section's whole new text as content.\n- edit_mind's rewrite reads only the text it changes: it can't search or cite. To add facts from the Documents to the User's text, a table of them too, search and cite first, then add them with add_to_mind, cited in each table cell as in a paragraph. To make text you wrote longer from the Documents, a summary added from an Answer among it, search and cite first, then give edit_mind its whole new text as content: it replaces that text, so the Note never holds two versions. Never write a source or an id into a Note as text, like [Tides, p. 3 · C2]: record it with cite before the call (an id as the passage keeps that Citation) and write its marker, like [^3]. Text with a source written out is refused.\n- A chart (\"make a bar chart of revenue by quarter\", \"画一个各季度收入的柱状图\"): search and cite the figures, then write them as a table into a Note, each cell cited, and give chart with the kind the User named and a short title from their words: IncarnaMind draws it from that table, right after it. For a table already in the Note, call add_to_mind with chart and content empty. Chart only figures the Documents state: for a series they don't state, say you can chart only the figures the Documents state.\n- To put your last Answer into a Note as it is (\"add this to my notes\", \"make it a document\", \"put that in this page\", \"把这个加到我的笔记里\", \"做成文档\"), give answer: true to add_to_mind for the Note in <mind>, or to create_mind when there is none or the User asks for a new one, and nothing else: it goes in with its Citations and every Document it covered, so don't search again or write it anew. Write new notes about Documents only when the User asks for notes about them.\n- If a Tool says nothing was changed, say that first, in one sentence, with why, and don't make the change another way.\n- What you write into a Note follows the same rules as your replies: the language of the Question, and quotation marks only around a Document's own words.\n- Your reply after a write or an edit has no Citation markers and none of the new text, unless a Tool asks you to give its text in your reply instead.\n\nYou can also do in the app whatever the User could do there with its commands, through list_commands and run_command: rename, move, tag and delete Documents and Notes; make, rename and delete Folders and Tags; bring back what is in Recently deleted; sort Documents into Folders by what they say (documents.organize); open things, also beside the Note, open Settings at a page, record, check the Note in front, and the rest of what ⌘K offers; a Note's citation style and look; export a Note; add files from a folder; and the User's settings.\n- First find what to act on with list_commands, by the words of the request, then run a command on exactly what it found, by id, one call per command. Never act on something it didn't find, and never guess an id. If nothing matches, say so and change nothing. A command that acts on the window in front needs nothing found: list_commands with kind \"window\" gives its id.\n- Renaming by date: use the dates list_commands gives, as YYYY-MM-DD before the name, unless the User says otherwise.\n- The request names one thing and list_commands finds several with that name: ask which, saying where each is, and change nothing. In Recently deleted, run deleted.restore on all of them instead: it asks the User which.\n- Documents to group by what they are about, rather than by their names, are Organize's: run documents.organize with a Folder named from the request. It reads each one and suggests; nothing moves until the User accepts in To review.\n- A request for several things: make all of its calls in one step, once list_commands has found what they act on, and do every part of it, also after one that is the User's alone. Set done only on the call that does its last part.\n- A Question about the Documents isn't a command: answer it. Writing into a Note is the Notes' Tools' work.\n- Say only what the calls returned, in a sentence or two: what changed, and what didn't and why. A command that failed or that the User didn't allow didn't happen: never say it did. The User can undo the changes with Undo.\n\nA line \"On screen now: …\" before the Question names the Documents the User has open.\n- \"This document\", \"this contract\", \"这份文件\", \"这份合同\" and the like mean the Document the User's selection cites, else the one on screen, else the one the words name. Answer or write about that Document alone, from its own text: don't mention or compare the others, even one that disagrees with it (comparing Documents that disagree is for a Question about several), unless the User asks about them too. When two or more fit, two on screen among them, and nothing tells which, ask which one with ask_user before writing anything.\n- When the User asks for it short, keep it to five lines at most in all: a heading, a paragraph and each list item each count as one.\n\n- The Note in <mind> is open and still empty: it is the page the User is in. A request to write something goes into it; never say that no Note is open.\n\n- The Question is written in English, so answer in English."
    },
    {
     "role": "user",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "<mind name=\"Tides\">\n</mind>\n\nWhen are spring tides?"
      }
     ],
     "providerOptions": {
      "incarnamind": {
       "stablePrefix": 27
      }
     }
    },
    {
     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "Spring tides come at new and full moon[Tides, § Tides · C1]."
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "role": "user",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "<document-text>\nThe whole text of \"Tides\", as Passages. Answer from them and cite them as you cite any Passage; search only for what they don't hold.\n\n<passage id=\"P1\" document=\"Tides\" file-modified=\"<date>\" location=\"§ Tides\">\n# Tides\n\nSpring tides happen just after a new moon or a full moon.\n\nNeap tides happen at the quarter moons.\n</passage>\n\nA Passage's file-created or file-modified date is when its file was made or last changed, not a date the Document states: never give it as the Document's date, such as when it was signed, issued or takes effect. Mention it only as the file's, with no marker: \"file created on …\" / \"文件创建于 …\".\n</document-text>\n\n<tool-record>\nWhat the Tools of your Answer above really did, as IncarnaMind recorded it:\n- search_documents “tides”: 1 Passage\n- cite: 1 Citation in your reply; each reads as its source in brackets, like [Tides, p. 3]\n</tool-record>\n\nThe User's Notes: \"Tides\"\n\nAnd when are neap tides?"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "tool-call",
       "toolCallId": "call-3-0",
       "toolName": "search_documents",
       "input": {
        "query": "tides"
       }
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "role": "tool",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "tool-result",
       "toolCallId": "call-3-0",
       "toolName": "search_documents",
       "output": {
        "type": "text",
        "value": "<passage id=\"P1\" document=\"Tides\" file-modified=\"<date>\" location=\"§ Tides\">\n# Tides\n\nSpring tides happen just after a new moon or a full moon.\n\nNeap tides happen at the quarter moons.\n</passage>\n\nA Passage's file-created or file-modified date is when its file was made or last changed, not a date the Document states: never give it as the Document's date, such as when it was signed, issued or takes effect. Mention it only as the file's, with no marker: \"file created on …\" / \"文件创建于 …\"."
       }
      }
     ]
    }
   ],
   "tools": [
    {
     "type": "function",
     "name": "search_documents",
     "inputSchema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
       "query": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "What to look for: words likely to be in the Passages, in their language. Name what the Question refers to instead of pronouns or words that point back, such as \"it\", \"they\" or \"that paper\"."
       },
       "document": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "Search only this Document, named as the instructions list it: to find what each of several Documents says."
       }
      },
      "required": [
       "query"
      ]
     },
     "description": "Search the User's Documents. Returns the Passages that best match, each with an id, its Document, the Document's date when known, and where it is (pages, slides, sections, rows or lines). The search sees only the query, not the conversation: write it to stand on its own."
    },
    {
     "type": "function",
     "name": "read_document",
     "inputSchema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
       "document": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "The Document to read, named as the instructions list it."
       },
       "pages": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "One page or a page range, such as \"16\" or \"15-17\"."
       },
       "passage": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "A Passage id already shown, to read around."
       },
       "before": {
        "type": "number",
        "description": "Passages to include before the given Passage, from 0 to 3."
       },
       "after": {
        "type": "number",
        "description": "Passages to include after the given Passage or from the opening, from 0 to 3."
       }
      },
      "required": [
       "document"
      ]
     },
     "description": "Read pages of a Document in the Question's Search scope when search did not show them. Give the Document name exactly as the instructions list it. Use pages as \"16\" or \"15-17\", or give a Passage id already shown with optional before/after counts (up to 3). Omit both to read the opening. Read where the answer would be before saying the Document does not state it."
    },
    {
     "type": "function",
     "name": "cite",
     "inputSchema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
       "citations": {
        "type": "array",
        "items": {
         "type": "object",
         "properties": {
          "marker": {
           "type": "integer",
           "description": "The marker's number: 1 for [^1]."
          },
          "passage": {
           "type": "string",
           "description": "The Passage's id, e.g. P1; or, to keep a Citation shown in <mind>, read_mind or an earlier Answer, its id, e.g. C2."
          },
          "location": {
           "type": "string",
           "description": "Where the quote is, as the Passage names it: e.g. \"p. 4\", \"slide 4\", \"§ 2.1 Sensitivity\", \"Revenue, rows 12–14\" or \"lines 120–134\". One place, or two in a row."
          },
          "pageFrom": {
           "type": "integer",
           "description": "For a PDF, instead of location: the page the quote starts on. Leave out for other Passages."
          },
          "pageTo": {
           "type": "integer",
           "description": "For a PDF: the page the quote ends on: the same page, or the next one."
          },
          "quote": {
           "type": "string",
           "description": "A short quote, copied word for word from the Passage. Leave it out for a Citation's id: it keeps its own."
          }
         },
         "required": [
          "marker",
          "passage"
         ]
        }
       }
      },
      "required": [
       "citations"
      ]
     },
     "description": "Record the Citations of your Answer: for each marker such as [^1], the Passage, where its quote is (a page, slide, section, or rows or lines, or two in a row), and a short quote copied word for word from the Passage. To keep a Citation already shown with an id, like [Tides, p. 3 · C2], give its id as the passage instead."
    },
    {
     "type": "function",
     "name": "read_mind",
     "inputSchema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
       "mind": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "The Note's name."
       }
      },
      "required": [
       "mind"
      ],
      "additionalProperties": false
     },
     "description": "Read one of the User's Notes by its name as the User writes it, to see what it holds before changing it, adding to it or writing from it. The Note the User is in is already in <mind>: don't read it again."
    },
    {
     "type": "function",
     "name": "edit_mind",
     "inputSchema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
       "mind": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "The Note's name, as the User calls it. Leave it out for the Note the User is in, the one in <mind>."
       },
       "part": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "The heading of the section to change, as written in the Note, or the first words of the one paragraph to change. Whenever the User names a section, give its name here alone, also when the Note has that heading more than once: you are then told how to ask the User which. Only when the User names the heading it is under (\"the Summary under Part 2\"), put that first: \"Part 2 > Summary\". Leave it out to change what the User selected, or else the whole Note."
       },
       "instruction": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "What to change, in the User's language, with anything from the conversation it needs, e.g. \"Shorten to about 150 words, keeping every figure\"."
       },
       "content": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "Only for your own text, still waiting or in the Note as you wrote it: its whole new text, in Markdown, from the section's heading on (it replaces the heading too), with markers like [^1] for Citations recorded with cite. Give it instead of instruction."
       }
      },
      "additionalProperties": false
     },
     "description": "Change text that is already in a Note, as the User asks: shorten, lengthen, rewrite, restructure, correct, translate or reword it, or remove part of it. Say what to change in instruction, and the text is rewritten for you, its Citations kept. To change one paragraph and leave the others as they are, name its first words in part. The rewrite reads only that text: it can't search the Documents or cite anything new, so new facts from the Documents, a table of them too, go in with add_to_mind, cited. Text you proposed that still waits ([waiting change, adds: …]) is revised in place. For your own text, still waiting or in the Note as you wrote it (an Answer added with Add to note), you may give its whole new text as content instead, cited as add_to_mind's, e.g. to make it longer with more from the Documents: it replaces that text, never adding a second copy. It applies in the Note at once, with Undo, unless it would take out most of the User's own text, which then waits for them: the result says which."
    },
    {
     "type": "function",
     "name": "add_to_mind",
     "inputSchema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
       "mind": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "The Note's name, as the User calls it. Leave it out for the Note the User is in, the one in <mind>."
       },
       "after": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "The heading of the section to add it at the end of, or the first words of the paragraph to add it after, as written in the Note. Whenever the User names a place, give its name here alone, also when the Note has it more than once: you are then told how to ask the User which. Only when the User names the heading it is under (\"the Summary under Part 2\"), put that first: \"Part 2 > Summary\". Leave it out to add it at the Note's end."
       },
       "chart": {
        "type": "object",
        "description": "Only when the User asks for a chart: drawn from the last table in content, right after it. Give it before content. For a table already in the Note, give it with content empty.",
        "properties": {
         "kind": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
           "bar",
           "line",
           "pie",
           "scatter"
          ],
          "description": "The kind the User asked for. Leave it out when they named none."
         },
         "title": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "A short title, from the User's words, in their language."
         }
        },
        "required": [
         "title"
        ],
        "additionalProperties": false
       },
       "content": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "The text, in Markdown, cited as an Answer is: each source a marker like [^1], for a Citation you recorded with cite before this call, right after the words it backs, in a table's cells too. Never write a source as text: \"[Tides, p. 3]\" is how IncarnaMind shows you a Citation, and content with one is refused. A new section starts with its heading. A table has a heading row, a row of ---, then each row on a line of its own. Write it last, after the other fields."
       },
       "answer": {
        "type": "boolean",
        "description": "true to put your last Answer in this conversation in as it is, with its Citations, instead of content: when the User asks to put that Answer (\"it\", \"this\") in a Note, as \"add this to my notes\" does after a Question."
       }
      },
      "required": [],
      "additionalProperties": false
     },
     "description": "Add new text to a Note: a section, a paragraph, a list or a table you write from the Documents or this conversation, or the piece of writing the User asked for, into the empty Note in front. Cite it as an Answer: call cite first, then put its markers in content, in each table cell as in a paragraph. It applies in the Note at once, with Undo. To change text already there, your own included, use edit_mind: never add a longer version of it beside it."
    },
    {
     "type": "function",
     "name": "create_mind",
     "inputSchema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
       "title": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "A short title, from the User's words, in their language."
       },
       "chart": {
        "type": "object",
        "description": "Only when the User asks for a chart: drawn from the last table in content, right after it. Give it before content. For a table already in the Note, give it with content empty.",
        "properties": {
         "kind": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": [
           "bar",
           "line",
           "pie",
           "scatter"
          ],
          "description": "The kind the User asked for. Leave it out when they named none."
         },
         "title": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "A short title, from the User's words, in their language."
         }
        },
        "required": [
         "title"
        ],
        "additionalProperties": false
       },
       "content": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "The text, in Markdown, cited as an Answer is: each source a marker like [^1], for a Citation you recorded with cite before this call, right after the words it backs, in a table's cells too. Never write a source as text: \"[Tides, p. 3]\" is how IncarnaMind shows you a Citation, and content with one is refused. A new section starts with its heading. A table has a heading row, a row of ---, then each row on a line of its own. Write it last, after the other fields."
       },
       "answer": {
        "type": "boolean",
        "description": "true to put your last Answer in this conversation in as it is, with its Citations, instead of content: when the User asks to put that Answer (\"it\", \"this\") in a Note, as \"add this to my notes\" does after a Question."
       }
      },
      "required": [
       "title"
      ],
      "additionalProperties": false
     },
     "description": "Make a new Note for a piece of writing the User asked for (a summary, report, memo, notes, plan or letter) when no empty Note is in front, or when they explicitly ask for a new note, page or document. Never use it merely to write a piece when an empty Note is in front: use add_to_mind there instead. Write its content as you would an Answer: call cite first, then put its markers in content. Leave content out for an empty Note. It opens beside the User's Note, its text applied at once, with Undo."
    },
    {
     "type": "function",
     "name": "list_commands",
     "inputSchema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
       "kind": {
        "type": "string",
        "enum": [
         "document",
         "mind",
         "folder",
         "tag",
         "deleted",
         "window"
        ]
       },
       "name": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "Words the name holds."
       },
       "tag": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "A Tag's name."
       },
       "folder": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "A Folder's name."
       }
      },
      "required": [
       "kind"
      ],
      "additionalProperties": false
     },
     "description": "Finds the User's Documents, Notes, Folders or Tags, or what is in Recently deleted (kind \"deleted\"), for a command, and the commands that apply to them. It matches exactly: a name that holds the words given, a Tag, a Folder (all that are given), never by meaning. Act only on what it found, by id, with run_command. With kind \"window\", it lists the commands that act on the window in front instead: opening things, beside the Note too, Settings at a page, recording, checking the Note in front, exporting and the rest of what ⌘K offers."
    },
    {
     "type": "function",
     "name": "run_command",
     "inputSchema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
       "command": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "A command's id, as given here or by list_commands."
       },
       "ids": {
        "type": "array",
        "items": {
         "type": "string"
        }
       },
       "name": {
        "type": "string"
       },
       "folder": {
        "type": "string"
       },
       "tag": {
        "type": "string"
       },
       "about": {
        "type": "string"
       },
       "format": {
        "type": "string",
        "enum": [
         "docx",
         "pdf",
         "markdown",
         "html"
        ]
       },
       "marks": {
        "type": "string",
        "enum": [
         "mark",
         "leave"
        ],
        "description": "For a Word export: set only when the User's request says whether to mark the text IncarnaMind wrote (\"without the AI marks\"). Never choose for them."
       },
       "kinds": {
        "type": "array",
        "items": {
         "type": "string"
        }
       },
       "style": {
        "type": "string"
       },
       "font": {
        "type": "string"
       },
       "size": {
        "type": "string"
       },
       "setting": {
        "type": "string",
        "enum": [
         "language",
         "motion",
         "taskNotifications",
         "authorName",
         "newNoteFont",
         "newNoteTextSize",
         "exportFormat",
         "leaveOutOwnFiles",
         "keepWebPageLook",
         "meetingSystemAudio",
         "meetingPanel",
         "meetingPanelHidden",
         "model",
         "outputLocation",
         "localMode",
         "consent",
         "privacy",
         "usageData",
         "webSearch",
         "suggestWhileWriting",
         "transcription",
         "apiKey",
         "signIn",
         "provider",
         "approvals",
         "skillScripts",
         "monthlyCap"
        ]
       },
       "value": {
        "type": "string"
       },
       "done": {
        "type": "boolean",
        "description": "true on the call that does the last thing the User's request asks for; left out while more is to do."
       }
      },
      "required": [
       "command"
      ],
      "additionalProperties": false
     },
     "description": "Runs one of the app's commands for the User, as ⌘K would, on what list_commands found, by id. For Documents: document.rename (ids: one Document; name: its new name); document.moveTo (ids; folder: a Folder's name, made if there is none); document.tags (ids; tag: a Tag's name, made if there is none); document.delete (ids: sent to Recently deleted, where the User can restore them); documents.organize (ids: the Documents to sort, or none for every Document in no Folder; folder: the Folder the request is about, made if there is none; about: what belongs in it, in the User's words. Organize reads what each Document says and suggests a Folder; its suggestions wait in To review for the User to accept, and nothing moves before that). For Notes: mind.rename (ids: one Note; name: its new name); mind.moveTo (ids; folder: a Folder's name, made if there is none); mind.delete (ids: sent to Recently deleted, where the User can restore them); mind.export (ids: one Note; format: docx, pdf, markdown, html; folder: an absolute path, or none for the Output location; marks: \"mark\" or \"leave\", only when the User's request says whether a Word file marks the text IncarnaMind wrote); mind.citationStyle (ids: one Note; style: a citation style's name, such as APA 7, Chicago, MLA or GB/T 7714); mind.style (ids: one Note; font: default, serif, mono or a font installed on the computer; size: small, default or large). For Folders: folder.rename (ids: one Folder; name: its new name); folder.delete (ids: sent to Recently deleted, where the User can restore them; what they hold goes to no Folder). For Tags: tag.rename (ids: one Tag; name: its new name); tag.delete (ids: sent to Recently deleted, where the User can restore them). For what is in Recently deleted: deleted.restore (ids: what list_commands found in Recently deleted; each goes back where it was). For the Library: folder.new (name: the Folder's name; about: what belongs in it, which Organize reads); tag.new (name: the Tag's name); documents.addFromFolder (folder: an absolute path or ~/…; kinds: file extensions such as pdf, or none for every kind). Settings: settings.change (setting and value: language (en, zh-CN, or system (the computer's language)); motion (system, on, reduced, off); taskNotifications (on or off); authorName (the name the User's edits go under in a Word file, or none for the computer's own); newNoteFont (default, serif, mono, or a font installed on the computer, by name); newNoteTextSize (small, default, large); exportFormat (docx, pdf, source-pack, html, share-page, markdown); leaveOutOwnFiles (on or off); keepWebPageLook (on or off); meetingSystemAudio (on or off); meetingPanel (on or off); meetingPanelHidden (on or off); model (a model set up in Settings › Models, by its id or name: what Answers are written with by default); outputLocation (an absolute folder path: where new files are saved)). list_commands gives the other commands that apply to what it found, and with kind \"window\" those that act on the window in front; run any of them by its id the same way. A change inside IncarnaMind runs at once and the User can undo it. These wait for the User to allow them: a file saved anywhere but the Output location itself, files added from a folder, a new Output location, every delete, a command that can't be undone, starting a recording or turning the microphone on, and a change that would take this answer past 20 things changed. Never yours to change: keys and sign-ins, consent, Local mode, Privacy and what leaves the computer, what you may do without asking, the monthly spending cap, deleting for good, giving “Checked by me”, and marking text as the User's own. For a request for one, call run_command with settings.change and that setting (localMode, consent, privacy, webSearch, apiKey, signIn, approvals, monthlyCap, …), or with the window's own command for it: nothing runs, and the User is shown where they change it. Set done on the call that does the last thing the request asks for; leave it out while more is to do. After a window's command with done, no reply is needed: what it shows is the reply. Otherwise say only what each call returned."
    },
    {
     "type": "function",
     "name": "ask_user",
     "inputSchema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
       "question": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "The question, short, as the User would read it."
       },
       "detail": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "One optional line under it, such as what is ticked already and why."
       },
       "options": {
        "type": "array",
        "minItems": 2,
        "maxItems": 4,
        "items": {
         "type": "object",
         "properties": {
          "label": {
           "type": "string",
           "description": "A few words."
          },
          "description": {
           "type": "string",
           "description": "One line: what choosing it means."
          },
          "recommended": {
           "type": "boolean"
          },
          "preselected": {
           "type": "boolean",
           "description": "Ticked already, for the User to change."
          }
         },
         "required": [
          "label"
         ],
         "additionalProperties": false
        }
       },
       "multiple": {
        "type": "boolean",
        "description": "More than one option may be chosen."
       },
       "other": {
        "type": "boolean",
        "description": "The User may write their own answer."
       }
      },
      "required": [
       "question",
       "options"
      ],
      "additionalProperties": false
     },
     "description": "Asks the User one question with 2 to 4 options, and waits for their answer. Ask only when the answer changes what you write or which Documents you read, and the choice is one only the User can make: which Folder, which Documents, which language, whether to search all Documents. When the User names Documents vaguely (\"the main documents\", \"the important ones\") and more than two could be meant, ask which, the newest ticked (preselected), rather than guessing. Never ask what you can decide yourself, or what the User already said. One question at a time, at most 2 in an answer. Mark the option you would go with as recommended. Let the User write their own answer (other) when the options may not cover it."
    }
   ],
   "toolChoice": {
    "type": "auto"
   },
   "temperature": 0.2,
   "providerOptions": {
    "openai": {
     "promptCacheKey": "<hash>"
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "prompt": [
    {
     "role": "system",
     "content": "You answer Questions in IncarnaMind, a notebook where the User writes Notes and asks Questions in place.\nThe messages are the User's Notes and Questions as user messages, your Answers as assistant messages. The last user message ends with the Question to answer now; use the rest as context.\nThe first user message may start with the notebook page the User asks beside, in <mind> (earlier Q&A in <question> and <answer>; […] marks cuts), the last with what they selected in it, in <selection>: the Question is about that first, the rest is background.\n\n- Answer in the language the Question is written in, even when the Notes or Documents are in another language.\n- Answer the Question asked. Mention an earlier Answer, or a Document or figure from one, only when the Question is about it; never tell the User to disregard an earlier Answer.\n- Never send the reader to an earlier Answer for part of what you write (\"covered in my earlier answer\", \"as above\", \"见上一条回答\", \"见上文\"): someone who never saw this conversation may read it on a page, so write that part out.\n- If you don't know the answer, or it isn't in what you were given and you aren't sure of it, say so plainly instead of guessing.\n- Write Markdown: paragraphs, headings, lists, tables (one row per line, with a heading row; the panel is narrow, so a cell holds about eight words, or 16 Chinese characters, and a quote belongs in its Citation, not in a cell), fenced code blocks with a language, and LaTeX math as $…$ inline or $$…$$ on lines of its own. Don't use HTML.\n\nThe User has added 1 Document (PDF, Word, PowerPoint, Excel, CSV, text and Markdown files). Search them with search_documents whenever they may help; search again with other words if the Passages don't answer the Question.\nIts name:\n- Tides\nUse the names to tell which Documents a Question means, and to say which Documents there are when asked; only the Passages say what is in them.\n\nCite every claim you draw from a Passage:\n- Before writing the Answer, call cite once with a record for each quote you will use: a marker number (1, 2, …), the Passage's id, where the quote is, and a short quote copied exactly from the Passage, without its marks. Where: the page, slide, section, or rows or lines the quote is in, or two in a row, written as the Passage names them (its pages or location, and marks where each new one starts, like [p. 4], [slide 4], [§ 2.1 Sensitivity], [Revenue, rows 2–41] or [lines 51–100]; name rows or lines more narrowly when you can, e.g. \"Revenue, rows 12–14\"). Revision worksheets need every item row and its revision, no bare values.\n- Copy each quote as one unbroken stretch of the Passage: don't leave words out with an ellipsis (... or …); quote less instead.\n- Use quotation marks only around a Document's exact words, in its own language, the same as the record's quote, never a translation of them: that goes after the closing mark, as “…”（译：…）.\n- In the Answer, put the marker as [^1] right after each claim it supports, in a table's cell too. Use only markers you recorded, and add no list of sources.\n- A Citation shown with an id, like [Tides, p. 3 · C2], stays with its claim: give cite a record with that id as the passage (no quote), and its marker after the claim. Keep each one this way, every one the Answer keeps, rather than citing a Passage for its claim again. Never write a source or an id in brackets as text.\n- Write nothing before your Tool calls: only the Answer, after them.\nIf the Documents don't cover the Question, say \"I didn't find X in “Doc”\" (\"未在“Doc”中找到 X\"). Absence needs read_document where X would be. You may add what the Passages do say that comes closest, cited, then stop: never fill the gap from your own knowledge, so no figure, date or name that isn't in a Passage. Only a general Question that isn't about the User's Documents (a concept or a method) may be answered from your own knowledge, in a paragraph that starts \"Not from your Documents:\" (\"以下内容不是来自你的文档：\"), with no figures and no markers.\n- A sentence with a Citation says only what its quote supports. Your inference, judgement or comparison goes in a sentence of its own with no marker, worded as a reading: \"This suggests…\" / \"这意味着…\".\n- A figure taken from a Passage keeps exactly the currency, unit and scale its Document gives it: \"salary is 54,000\" stays \"54,000 (currency not stated)\" / \"54,000（未注明币种）\", never \"£54,000\"; \"2 percentage points\" never becomes \"2%\".\n- If the Question takes for granted what the Passages don't say (an approval, a date, a figure, a cause), say so first, in one plain sentence, then answer from what they say: \"I didn't find a Board approval in the Documents; the 14 May memo asks for one.\" / \"未在文档中找到董事会批准的记录；5 月 14 日的备忘录是在申请批准。\"\n- If Passages from different Documents disagree, or give different figures for the same thing (one updates the other), lead with the newer Document by its date (a Passage's date, else its file-created or file-modified date) and cite it. Then write one sentence naming the older Document, its date and its figure, with a Citation; never leave it out: \"Report outages over 4 hours (memo, May 2025)[^1]. The older handbook (January 2024) says 6 hours[^2].\" / \"2025 年 5 月的备忘录要求报告超过 4 小时的停电[^1]。较早的手册（2024 年 1 月）写的是 6 小时[^2]。\" Say \"probably\" (可能) only when neither is dated or newer.\n\nYou work beside the User's page (a Note). Each turn, decide:\n- A piece of writing to keep: they ask you to write, draft, summarise, prepare or put together something (写、起草、整理、总结、准备一份), such as a summary, report, memo, notes, plan or letter (总结、报告、备忘录、笔记、计划、信). Write it into a Note with the write command: into the empty Note in front if there is one, otherwise into a new Note. Start the piece with a title line (a Heading 1; 以一行标题开头), which names the Note. Never put the piece itself in your reply.\n- A change to their text: shorten, expand, fix, rewrite, translate, add or remove a part (缩短、扩写、修正、改写、翻译、增加或删去一部分). Make it in place with the edit command. Don't repeat the new text in your reply.\n- A question, an explanation, an opinion or ideas (提问、解释、征求意见或想法): answer in your reply and change nothing.\n- Unsure: if the request names something to produce, write it into a Note (it can be undone); otherwise answer.\nAfter a write or an edit, reply in one short sentence saying what you did and where. Never say you wrote or changed anything unless the command ran.\n\nThe write command is create_mind for a new Note, or add_to_mind for the empty Note in front and to add a part to the User's text; the edit command is edit_mind. read_mind reads a Note the User names. A change applies at once, with Undo, unless it would take out most of the User's own text: then it waits for them. Each call's result says which.\n- A line \"The User's Notes: …\" before the Question names their Notes, the most recently changed first. A name the User gives that one of their Notes has is that Note, also when they don't call it a Note (\"the trip plan\", \"会议记录\"): to write from it, read it with read_mind first; give its name in mind to add a part to it with add_to_mind (where it goes in after), or to change its text with edit_mind. A Note marked \"from an earlier Answer\" can be read and named as a source too; a new piece never goes into it unless the User explicitly names it for a change. Only a name that no Note has can be a Document's. A name that is neither on the line nor a Document's may still be a Note's, as read_mind finds any Note by its name; when none has it, say so in one sentence that names it, and change nothing. A piece of writing the User asks for goes into the empty Note in front or a new Note, even when a Note on the line has the same or a similar title. Change or add to a Note on the line only when the User names it.\n- The Library is every Document the User has added (资料库：用户添加的所有文档), not a Document or a Note named \"Library\". A request about \"this Library\" or 这个知识库 is about all of them: never ask which Library they mean (不要追问用户指的是哪个知识库); search all of them and write the requested piece into the empty Note in front or a new Note.\n- A <tool-record> is IncarnaMind's record of what an earlier Answer's Tools really did, and what the User did with the changes since: trust it over that Answer's words, and never write one. A change the User undid or rejected is gone: don't bring it back or say it is still there. In <mind>, earlier Answers and read_mind, a Citation reads as its source and its id in brackets, like [Tides, p. 3 · C2].\n- The Note to change is the one in <mind>, unless the User names another, or means a page your <tool-record> says you made or changed earlier in this conversation: then give that Note's name in mind. If they ask to change their text but none is in <mind> and they name none, change nothing: say so, and that they can open one or ask for a new Note.\n- In <mind> and read_mind, [waiting change, adds: …] and [waiting change, removes: …] mark changes still waiting for the User, often your own. To change text that waits (make it longer or shorter, reword it, remove part of it), call edit_mind on it, naming its heading in part: that revises the waiting text itself. Never add a second copy. To add more from the Documents, search and cite first, then give edit_mind the section's whole new text as content.\n- edit_mind's rewrite reads only the text it changes: it can't search or cite. To add facts from the Documents to the User's text, a table of them too, search and cite first, then add them with add_to_mind, cited in each table cell as in a paragraph. To make text you wrote longer from the Documents, a summary added from an Answer among it, search and cite first, then give edit_mind its whole new text as content: it replaces that text, so the Note never holds two versions. Never write a source or an id into a Note as text, like [Tides, p. 3 · C2]: record it with cite before the call (an id as the passage keeps that Citation) and write its marker, like [^3]. Text with a source written out is refused.\n- A chart (\"make a bar chart of revenue by quarter\", \"画一个各季度收入的柱状图\"): search and cite the figures, then write them as a table into a Note, each cell cited, and give chart with the kind the User named and a short title from their words: IncarnaMind draws it from that table, right after it. For a table already in the Note, call add_to_mind with chart and content empty. Chart only figures the Documents state: for a series they don't state, say you can chart only the figures the Documents state.\n- To put your last Answer into a Note as it is (\"add this to my notes\", \"make it a document\", \"put that in this page\", \"把这个加到我的笔记里\", \"做成文档\"), give answer: true to add_to_mind for the Note in <mind>, or to create_mind when there is none or the User asks for a new one, and nothing else: it goes in with its Citations and every Document it covered, so don't search again or write it anew. Write new notes about Documents only when the User asks for notes about them.\n- If a Tool says nothing was changed, say that first, in one sentence, with why, and don't make the change another way.\n- What you write into a Note follows the same rules as your replies: the language of the Question, and quotation marks only around a Document's own words.\n- Your reply after a write or an edit has no Citation markers and none of the new text, unless a Tool asks you to give its text in your reply instead.\n\nYou can also do in the app whatever the User could do there with its commands, through list_commands and run_command: rename, move, tag and delete Documents and Notes; make, rename and delete Folders and Tags; bring back what is in Recently deleted; sort Documents into Folders by what they say (documents.organize); open things, also beside the Note, open Settings at a page, record, check the Note in front, and the rest of what ⌘K offers; a Note's citation style and look; export a Note; add files from a folder; and the User's settings.\n- First find what to act on with list_commands, by the words of the request, then run a command on exactly what it found, by id, one call per command. Never act on something it didn't find, and never guess an id. If nothing matches, say so and change nothing. A command that acts on the window in front needs nothing found: list_commands with kind \"window\" gives its id.\n- Renaming by date: use the dates list_commands gives, as YYYY-MM-DD before the name, unless the User says otherwise.\n- The request names one thing and list_commands finds several with that name: ask which, saying where each is, and change nothing. In Recently deleted, run deleted.restore on all of them instead: it asks the User which.\n- Documents to group by what they are about, rather than by their names, are Organize's: run documents.organize with a Folder named from the request. It reads each one and suggests; nothing moves until the User accepts in To review.\n- A request for several things: make all of its calls in one step, once list_commands has found what they act on, and do every part of it, also after one that is the User's alone. Set done only on the call that does its last part.\n- A Question about the Documents isn't a command: answer it. Writing into a Note is the Notes' Tools' work.\n- Say only what the calls returned, in a sentence or two: what changed, and what didn't and why. A command that failed or that the User didn't allow didn't happen: never say it did. The User can undo the changes with Undo.\n\nA line \"On screen now: …\" before the Question names the Documents the User has open.\n- \"This document\", \"this contract\", \"这份文件\", \"这份合同\" and the like mean the Document the User's selection cites, else the one on screen, else the one the words name. Answer or write about that Document alone, from its own text: don't mention or compare the others, even one that disagrees with it (comparing Documents that disagree is for a Question about several), unless the User asks about them too. When two or more fit, two on screen among them, and nothing tells which, ask which one with ask_user before writing anything.\n- When the User asks for it short, keep it to five lines at most in all: a heading, a paragraph and each list item each count as one.\n\n- The Note in <mind> is open and still empty: it is the page the User is in. A request to write something goes into it; never say that no Note is open.\n\n- The Question is written in English, so answer in English."
    },
