        "value": "Recorded [^1]. Write the Answer now if you haven't yet; don't repeat it."
       }
      }
     ]
    }
   ],
   "tools": [
    {
     "type": "function",
     "name": "search_documents",
     "inputSchema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
       "query": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "What to look for: words likely to be in the Passages, in their language. Name what the Question refers to instead of pronouns or words that point back, such as \"it\", \"they\" or \"that paper\"."
       },
       "document": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "Search only this Document, named as the instructions list it: to find what each of several Documents says."
       }
      },
      "required": [
       "query"
      ]
     },
     "description": "Search the User's Documents. Returns the Passages that best match, each with an id, its Document, the Document's date when known, and where it is (pages, slides, sections, rows or lines). The search sees only the query, not the conversation: write it to stand on its own. The Documents are in English. Search finds Passages best in their own language: when the Question is in another language than the Documents it may be about, search with the query translated into their language too. Only the query is in their language: write the reply in the Question's."
    },
    {
     "type": "function",
     "name": "cite",
     "inputSchema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
       "citations": {
        "type": "array",
        "items": {
         "type": "object",
         "properties": {
          "marker": {
           "type": "integer",
           "description": "The marker's number: 1 for [^1]."
          },
          "passage": {
           "type": "string",
           "description": "The Passage's id, e.g. P1; or, to keep a Citation shown in <mind>, read_mind or an earlier Answer, its id, e.g. C2."
          },
          "location": {
           "type": "string",
           "description": "Where the quote is, as the Passage names it: e.g. \"p. 4\", \"slide 4\", \"§ 2.1 Sensitivity\", \"Revenue, rows 12–14\" or \"lines 120–134\". One place, or two in a row."
          },
          "pageFrom": {
           "type": "integer",
           "description": "For a PDF, instead of location: the page the quote starts on. Leave out for other Passages."
          },
          "pageTo": {
           "type": "integer",
           "description": "For a PDF: the page the quote ends on: the same page, or the next one."
          },
          "quote": {
           "type": "string",
           "description": "A short quote, copied word for word from the Passage. Leave it out for a Citation's id: it keeps its own."
          }
         },
         "required": [
          "marker",
          "passage"
         ]
        }
       }
      },
      "required": [
       "citations"
      ]
     },
     "description": "Record the Citations of your Answer: for each marker such as [^1], the Passage, where its quote is (a page, slide, section, or rows or lines, or two in a row), and a short quote copied word for word from the Passage. To keep a Citation already shown with an id, like [Tides, p. 3 · C2], give its id as the passage instead."
    },
    {
     "type": "function",
     "name": "calculate",
     "inputSchema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
       "figures": {
        "type": "array",
        "items": {
         "type": "object",
         "properties": {
          "marker": {
           "type": "integer",
           "description": "The figure's marker number: 1 for [=1]. Figures are numbered apart from Citations."
          },
          "label": {
           "type": "string",
           "description": "What the result is, with its period: \"Revenue growth 2025\"."
          },
          "inputs": {
           "type": "array",
           "items": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
             "name": {
              "type": "string",
              "description": "A short name for the formula: rev2024."
             },
             "label": {
              "type": "string",
              "description": "What it is, with its year or period: \"Revenue 2024\"."
             },
             "citation": {
              "type": "integer",
              "description": "The marker of a Citation record with the number in its quote: 1 for [^1]. Or give passage, location and quote instead."
             },
             "passage": {
              "type": "string",
              "description": "Instead of citation: the Passage's id, e.g. P1."
             },
             "location": {
              "type": "string",
              "description": "With passage: where the quote is, as for a Citation."
             },
             "quote": {
              "type": "string",
              "description": "With passage: a short quote holding the number, copied word for word."
             },
             "number": {
              "type": "string",
              "description": "The number as it is written in the quote: \"1.2亿\", \"$1,482.6 million\"."
             },
             "position": {
              "type": "integer",
              "description": "Instead of number, when the number is in the quote twice: its place among the quote's numbers, 1 for the first."
             },
             "unitQuote": {
              "type": "string",
              "description": "When the number's scale or currency is only in a table's heading or caption (\"单位：万元\", \"in millions\"): that line, copied word for word from the same Passage."
             },
             "reading": {
              "type": "integer",
              "description": "Only when told the number can be read more than one way: the reading to use, as numbered then."
             },
             "rate": {
              "type": "string",
              "description": "Only for an exchange rate: the currencies it converts, as \"CNY per USD\"."
             },
             "bracketedCost": {
              "type": "boolean",
              "description": "True when the number is a cost or deduction printed in brackets (\"Finance costs (41.2)\", \"财务费用 (41.2)\"), so its size is meant. Never for a loss or another result in brackets."
             }
            },
            "required": [
             "name"
            ]
           }
          },
          "formula": {
           "type": "string",
           "description": "In Excel's syntax, over the inputs' names: (rev2025 - rev2024) / rev2024, SUM(q1, q2, q3, q4), RRI(4, start, end), a > b. A percent is a share, 25% is 0.25: never multiply by 100, give as instead. Inputs are in base units: never multiply or divide by 1000 or 10000 to change the scale, give scale instead."
          },
          "as": {
           "type": "string",
           "enum": [
            "number",
            "percent",
            "points",
            "currency",
            "times",
            "yes-no"
           ],
           "description": "How to show the result: percent for a growth rate or a share. Default: from the inputs."
          },
          "decimals": {
           "type": "integer",
           "description": "Decimal places, when the default won't do."
          },
          "scale": {
           "type": "string",
           "description": "Show an amount in this scale: 万, 亿, thousand, million, billion. Inputs are in base units with their page's scale applied, so this, never the formula, sets the scale shown."
          },
          "stated": {
           "type": "string",
           "description": "The number as the sentence to check writes it: \"1.1x\", \"12%\"."
          },
          "relation": {
           "type": "string",
           "enum": [
            "equals",
            "at most",
            "at least"
           ],
           "description": "How the figure stands to stated: \"equals\" when stated is the figure; \"at most\" or \"at least\" when stated is a limit the sentence says the figure stays within or above (\"inside a 3.25x covenant\", \"不超过3.25倍\", \"至少5倍\")."
          }
         },
         "required": [
          "marker",
          "inputs",
          "formula",
          "stated",
          "relation"
         ]
        }
       }
      },
      "required": [
       "figures"
      ]
     },
     "description": "Work out a number from numbers in the Passages instead of in your head, and get its result back. Give figures, each {marker, label, inputs, formula, as}. Each input is {name, label, citation, number}: citation is the marker of a cite record whose quote has the number (1 for [^1]), or give passage, location and quote instead; number is the number as it is written there. Revision worksheets need every item row and its revision, no bare values. The formula is in Excel's syntax over the inputs' names. For example: {\"figures\": [{\"marker\": 1, \"label\": \"Revenue growth 2025\", \"inputs\": [{\"name\": \"r24\", \"label\": \"Revenue 2024\", \"citation\": 1, \"number\": \"$1,231.0 million\"}, {\"name\": \"r25\", \"label\": \"Revenue 2025\", \"citation\": 1, \"number\": \"$1,482.6 million\"}], \"formula\": \"r25 / r24 - 1\", \"as\": \"percent\"}]}. IncarnaMind reads each number from its quote, checks the quote and does the arithmetic. Inputs are read in base units with their page's scale (\"单位：万元\", \"in millions\") applied, so give scale (\"亿\", \"billion\") for the scale a figure is shown in, never multiply or divide by 1000 or 10000 in the formula. An input keeps its sign as the Passage writes it: an amount in brackets, \"(12,500)\", is negative, so take a deduction off by adding it, never by subtracting it. Give stated, the number as the sentence to check writes it: IncarnaMind compares the result with it. When that number is a limit the sentence says the figure stays within or above (\"leverage inside the 3.25x covenant\"), give relation \"at most\" or \"at least\"."
    }
   ],
   "toolChoice": {
    "type": "auto"
   }
  }
 ]
}
